<h1 class="article-title">Norwegian Experience in Sustainable Restoration of Contaminated Waterbeds Following Remediation</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>At a knowledge-sharing seminar on 3 June entitled “Reducing and Preventing Pollution: Effective Environmental Management Systems in Industrial Waterways”, representatives of the Norwegian Institute for Water Research (NIVA) shared both successful and less successful experiences of natural recovery in contaminated waterbodies following the partial removal of polluted sediments. Experts from Latvia’s State Environmental Service, Liepāja City Council and the Liepāja Special Economic Zone Authority also discussed pollution reduction. The event was organised under the “Remediation of the Historically Contaminated Site in the Karosta Canal of the Port of Liepāja” project, implemented by the Liepāja SEZ Authority in cooperation with NIVA and the Baltic Coasts association.</p><p>NIVA research director Marianne Olsen presented “Contaminated Sediments in Norway”, describing the country’s approach to contaminated sediment management. Norway has mapped polluted sites since 1998, while its 2007 National Action Plan identified 17 priority remediation sites in fjords and ports. Priorities were based on environmental and human-health risks. Common remediation methods include dredging contaminated sediments where required to maintain navigation depth, depositing sediments on land or at designated marine sites with containment, and covering cleaned seabeds with thin layers of active or inert materials to support sustainable recovery.</p><p>NIVA researcher Sigurd Øxnevad presented “Environmental Monitoring of a Small Fjord (Vikkilen) in Southern Norway in 2021 — Five Years After Remediation Actions”, outlining a successful case of sustainable environmental recovery.</p><p>He described Vikkilen fjord, home to a shipyard since 1946. Shipyard operations released tributyltin (TBT), polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons and heavy metals into the fjord. Much of the pollution came from antifouling ship paints and sandblasting, with sediment TBT concentrations reaching 93 mg/kg. The relevant chemicals were banned worldwide in 2008. Remediation took place in 2014–2016, removing most of the contamination from the seabed and covering it with geotextile, sand and gravel. Regular monitoring subsequently assessed benthic fauna and chemical concentrations in sediment, blue mussels and snails. Pollution had particularly harmed snails, affecting their reproduction and population recovery. Their condition improved by 2016, and since then no snails showing pollution-related harm have been found.</p><p>NIVA senior researcher Hilde Cecilie Trannum presented “Long-term Biological Effects of Capping with Thin Layers of Activated Carbon in the Grenland Fjords, Norway”, discussing a less successful example that provides important insight into natural processes and human impacts.</p><p>The researcher explained that benthic habitats contain polychaete worms, molluscs, echinoderms, crustaceans and many other organisms. The Grenland fjords have a long history of dioxin and mercury pollution from magnesium factories that operated for fifty years until 2002. In two test areas, Ormerfjord at approximately 30 metres depth and Eidangerfjord at approximately 95 metres, a thin layer of powdered activated carbon mixed with clay was applied to the seabed.</p><p>Chemical and ecological monitoring was conducted one month, one year, four years and nine years after sediment removal and activated-carbon capping. Chemical analyses showed improvements, including reduced dioxin dispersion and accumulation and up to 90% lower dioxin uptake by mussels and polychaetes. Surprisingly, biodiversity suffered, especially among echinoderms, whose benthic populations fell by half. This and other examples demonstrate the importance of carefully choosing a site-specific approach to ecological recovery after contaminated sediments are removed.</p><p>Liepāja City Council Environment Department head Dace Liepniece presented “Sustainable Environmental Management and Restoration of Degraded Areas: Liepāja City Council’s Experience”. She described the rehabilitation of Zirgu Island, the largest of the 15 islands in Lake Liepāja. Developed in stages for active recreation, the site now provides 17,550 m² of improved public space, including an 824-metre walking trail with viewing platforms, rest areas and outdoor exercise equipment.</p><p>Dace Liepniece also reported on work addressing a component of the Karosta Canal pollution hotspot listed by the Baltic Marine Environment Protection Commission (HELCOM): untreated wastewater entering the Baltic Sea through the canal. Between 2006 and 2009, Cohesion Fund support was used to eliminate untreated wastewater discharges into the canal and sea, collect wastewater from Karosta and divert it to treatment systems, and construct or reconstruct water-supply and sewer pipelines to enable environmentally sound wastewater management.</p><p>State Environmental Service project implementation group head Madara Zingere presented “Contaminated Site Management Systems: Opportunities to Be Seized”. She described improvements to the register of contaminated and potentially contaminated sites under the project “Digital Transformation of the Contaminated Site Management Model”, also called “PPPV Digitalisation” (No. LV-CLIMATE-0006).</p><p>The PPPV register currently lists 3,603 contaminated or potentially contaminated sites: 263 contaminated, 2,606 potentially contaminated and 734 non-contaminated sites (surveyed or fully remediated). Information is presented as a list and on a map with municipality, parish and waterbody layers. Established in 2004, the current register has a complex and fragmented management process, partly paper-based information flows and gaps in up-to-date information.</p><p>A digital contaminated-site management system is planned to support high-quality identification, investigation and remediation of contaminated locations, improving soil, ground, groundwater and surface-water quality, restoring the environment at and around the sites, and preventing risks to public health and nature.</p><p>The seminar’s final presentation was given by Liepāja SEZ remediation expert Pēteris Blumats. Entitled “Remediation and Environmental Monitoring: Reducing Environmental Impacts”, it outlined the environmental risks and planned activities of the Karosta Canal remediation project.</p><p>Latvia’s Climate Change Adaptation Plan to 2030 identifies several risks for Liepāja. Rapid changes in sea level can wash accumulated pollutants from the slopes of the Karosta Canal into the water as an oil film, potentially carrying them into the open Baltic Sea.</p><p>Accordingly, a monitored natural recovery approach is planned for the Karosta Canal until 2035, following remediation works under this project. These works envisage removing 202,247 m³ of sediment that does not meet statutory standards and contains 400 tonnes of contaminants. The aim is to establish the ecological conditions needed for sustainable environmental development.</p><p>Monitored natural recovery, or self-purification, relies on natural biodegradation processes that reduce the bioavailability or toxicity of pollutants in sediment.</p><p>Long-term monitoring will continue during and after remediation of the Karosta Canal, covering surface water, sediments (chemical and biological parameters) and groundwater.</p><p>The project implementation period runs from 1 August 2021 to 31 December 2023.</p><p>The project budget is EUR 5,117,647.05, including EUR 4,350,000.00 in co-financing from the Norwegian Financial Mechanism.</p><p> </p><p>“Working together for a green Europe”</p></div>
